{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '442,668p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v442; sed -n '1133,1204p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1133; tail -25 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790808000000-orchestrator-linker-over-dht-owner-decisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '442,668p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v442; sed -n '1133,1204p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md | nl -ba -v1133; tail -25 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790808000000-orchestrator-linker-over-dht-owner-decisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"   442\t### 5.5 Replication: the ledger, the backlog, repair and the result\n   443\t\n   444\tThe node's store handle no longer asks the DHT to replicate at the moment of\n   445\ta put. A put inserts into `:local`, records the address on the node's ledger\n   446\tring, and returns the local verdict. Oversize is still refused before the\n   447\tlocal insert. `step` owns everything after that. No round waits, as before.\n   448\t\n   449\t#### 5.5.1 The publication ledger\n   450\t\n   451\tA **publication** is the set of distinct addresses put between two\n   452\tannouncements, closed by the announced manifest. The node keeps one ledger\n   453\tper live publication: every address of the publication, each in exactly one\n   454\tof three states.\n   455\t\n   456\t| State | Meaning |\n   457\t|---|---|\n   458\t| `waiting` | No outcome yet: the address is queued or its request is outstanding, for the first time in this publication. |\n   459\t| `sent` | A `/sent` fact arrived while this publication held the address. Never leaves this state. |\n   460\t| `failed` | The last outcome was not `/sent`. Carries that reason. May become `sent` by a later repair. |\n   461\t\n   462\t- The ledger is **complete and fixed**: `:blobs` is its size and never\n   463\t  changes. Repair updates this ledger; it never creates another publication\n   464\t  for the same manifest.\n   465\t- A publication is **live** from its announcement until it is retired.\n   466\t  It is retired when it is `:acknowledged`, when its repair ends (5.5.4),\n   467\t  or when the node closes.\n   468\t- **After the first report, every blob is `sent` or `failed`.** A failed\n   469\t  blob whose repair try is queued or outstanding stays `failed`, with its\n   470\t  last reason, until a `/sent` arrives. So in every event of a publication\n   471\t  `:sent + (count :failed) = :blobs`.\n   472\t\n   473\t**Shared addresses.** One address has at most one queue entry and at most\n   474\tone outstanding request on the node, whatever number of live publications\n   475\thold it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- When an address's request settles, the outcome is written to the ledger\n   478\t  of **every live publication** that holds the address as `waiting` or\n   479\t  `failed`: `/sent` makes it `sent` in each; a failure makes it `failed`\n   480\t  with that reason in each.\n   481\t- A publication announced while an address it holds is already queued or\n   482\t  outstanding joins that one attempt.\n   483\t- Nothing else is shared. An outcome recorded before a publication was\n   484\t  announced does not count for it: its address is admitted and requested\n   485\t  again. A write is idempotent by content address.\n   486\t\n   487\t#### 5.5.2 The backlog\n   488\t\n   489\tA node holds two FIFO queues of addresses awaiting a replicate request: the\n   490\t**fresh** queue, for a publication's first pass, and the **repair** queue,\n   491\tfor retries.\n   492\t\n   493\t- **Admission** of fresh addresses happens in `step`, when the ledger ring\n   494\t  is read: each put address is appended to the fresh tail in put order.\n   495\t- **Capacity.** The two queues together hold at most `:max-backlog`\n   496\t  addresses (default 4096). A fresh address that arrives when they are full\n   497\t  is **not admitted**: it is `failed` at once with reason\n   498\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full`. Its local copy stays. Nothing is evicted to\n   499\t  make room. A repair address that finds them full is not admitted either\n   500\t  and stays `failed` with the reason it had; the next repair cycle offers it\n   501\t  again.\n   502\t- **Release.** Each step appends replicate requests while the number\n   503\t  outstanding is below the DHT's `max-pending-writes`. The node never has\n   504\t  more outstanding than that bound, so its own writes never draw `/busy`.\n   505\t- **Fairness between the queues.** While the repair queue is non-empty,\n   506\t  `:repair-slots` (default 16) of the outstanding bound are reserved for\n   507\t  it: fresh requests hold at most `max-pending-writes − repair-slots`\n   508\t  outstanding, repair requests at most `:repair-slots`. While the repair\n   509\t  queue is empty, fresh requests may use the whole bound. Neither queue can\n   510\t  starve the other, and repairs never take more than their share from a new\n   511\t  publication.\n   512\t- **Order.** Each queue is strictly FIFO. Fresh order is put order, so an\n   513\t  older publication's first pass precedes a newer one's. No publication is\n   514\t  prioritized.\n   515\t- **Outcomes.** One request has one outcome. `/sent` is `sent`. Every\n   516\t  `/unacknowledged` reason is `failed` with that reason. There is no\n   517\t  immediate second try: a retry is a repair (5.5.4).\n   518\t- **A solo or non-publishing node holds no backlog.** Every put is `failed`\n   519\t  at admission with `/solo` or `/unpublished`.\n   520\t- **A lost ledger-ring entry** (more puts in one window than the ring\n   521\t  holds) or a facts gap reports `:publication-unknown`, as today, and that\n   522\t  publication is not live.\n   523\t\n   524\t#### 5.5.3 The result and the events\n   525\t\n   526\t```clojure\n   527\t{:dao.space.dht/event :published | :republished\n   528\t :manifest m\n   529\t :result :acknowledged | :partial | :unacknowledged\n   530\t :blobs n            ; the ledger's size; constant for the publication\n   531\t :sent s             ; ledger entries sent\n   532\t :peers p            ; fewest peers any sent blob reached; absent when s = 0\n   533\t :failed [{:address a :reason r :peers k} ...]   ; every entry not sent\n   534\t :repairing? b       ; true while the node will retry this publication\n   535\t :ended reason}      ; only when repair ended without acknowledgement\n   536\t```\n   537\t\n   538\t| `:result` | Condition, over the whole ledger | What it promises |\n   539\t|---|---|---|\n   540\t| `:acknowledged` | Every blob sent. | Every address of the publication was handed to at least `ack-peers` peers. |\n   541\t| `:partial` | The manifest blob sent; at least one other blob not. | Peers can find the publication. The addresses under `:failed` are held and served by the publisher only. |\n   542\t| `:unacknowledged` | The manifest blob not sent. | Nothing beyond the local copy. `:failed` still lists every blob not sent. |\n   543\t\n   544\t- The result is always computed over the **whole ledger**. A blob sent in\n   545\t  the first pass and a blob sent by a repair count alike. A publication\n   546\t  whose manifest was sent in the first pass and whose rows were sent by\n   547\t  repair is `:acknowledged`.\n   548\t- `:failed` is complete, in address order.\n   549\t- `:acknowledged?` is removed, with no compatibility path.\n   550\t\n   551\t**Which event, when.** A publication produces:\n   552\t\n   553\t| Event | When | Times |\n   554\t|---|---|---|\n   555\t| `:published` | The **first report**: every blob has left `waiting`. | Exactly once. |\n   556\t| `:republished` | A repair changed the `:result` (to `:partial` or to `:acknowledged`), or repair ended. A change that ends repair is one event. | Zero or more; at most two result changes and one end. |\n   557\t\n   558\t- `:repairing?` is true in an event exactly when the result is not\n   559\t  `:acknowledged` and repair has not ended.\n   560\t- A repair cycle that changes no result produces **no event**. A\n   561\t  publication that never improves is silent after its first report.\n   562\t- **Final acknowledgement** is the one `:republished` (or the first\n   563\t  `:published`) whose `:result` is `:acknowledged`. Nothing follows it.\n   564\t- `:ended` is one of `:dao.space.dht/terminal` (every remaining failure is\n   565\t  terminal), `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`, `:dao.space.dht/displaced` (5.5.4).\n   566\t\n   567\t#### 5.5.4 Automatic repair (owner decision 5: \"Automatic retry while open\")\n   568\t\n   569\tA publication whose first report is not `:acknowledged` is repaired by the\n   570\tnode, in the background, for as long as the node is open.\n   571\t\n   572\t- **What is retried.** A `failed` blob whose reason is **retryable**:\n   573\t  `/too-few-peers`, `/busy`, `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full`. A blob that is\n   574\t  `sent` is never sent again.\n   575\t- **What is not.** A **terminal** reason is never retried: `/solo`,\n   576\t  `/unpublished`, `/oversize`, `/absent`, `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`. A solo\n   577\t  or non-publishing node therefore repairs nothing: its composition cannot\n   578\t  change while it is open.\n   579\t- **A repair cycle.** Each repairing publication has a due reading, in the\n   580\t  node's own ticks. At a step whose reading has reached it, the\n   581\t  publication's retryable failed blobs that are not already queued or\n   582\t  outstanding are appended to the repair tail, oldest publication first,\n   583\t  address order within it, as the capacity of 5.5.2 allows.\n   584\t- **Pacing.** The first cycle is due `:repair-ticks` (default 30000) after\n   585\t  the first report. After a cycle in which no blob of the publication was\n   586\t  sent, the delay doubles, up to `:repair-max-ticks` (default 600000).\n   587\t  After a cycle in which one was, it returns to `:repair-ticks`. A tick gap\n   588\t  makes a cycle late, never wrong.\n   589\t- **Bounds.** At most `:max-repairing` (default 16) publications repair at\n   590\t  once. When one more needs repair, the oldest is retired with\n   591\t  `:ended :dao.space.dht/displaced`. With the backlog capacity and the\n   592\t  reserved slots, a node that can reach nobody holds a bounded ledger, a\n   593\t  bounded queue and a bounded number of requests, and says nothing after\n   594\t  each first report beyond one displacement event per publication retired.\n   595\t- **Repair ends** in exactly four ways:\n   596\t  1. the result becomes `:acknowledged` (the final event; no `:ended`);\n   597\t  2. no retryable failure remains and the result is not `:acknowledged`\n   598\t     (`:ended :dao.space.dht/terminal`). When this already holds at the\n   599\t     first report, that `:published` event carries it and\n   600\t     `:repairing? false`;\n   601\t  3. `cancel!` (5.5.5);\n   602\t  4. displacement.\n   603\t\n   604\t  And silently when the node closes: `close!` discards every ledger and\n   605\t  both queues and reports nothing. Local copies are durable. A node opened\n   606\t  again over the same directory repairs nothing from before; the ledger is\n   607\t  process state and is not rebuilt.\n   608\t- **A manual nudge.** `(dao.space.dht/retry! node manifest-address)` makes\n   609\t  that publication's next cycle due now and resets its delay. It changes\n   610\t  no rule above and reports nothing itself. It is refused for a manifest\n   611\t  that is not repairing.\n   612\t- `busy?` is true while a first report is owed or any address is queued or\n   613\t  outstanding. A publication waiting for its next cycle does not make the\n   614\t  node busy.\n   615\t\n   616\t#### 5.5.5 Cancellation\n   617\t\n   618\t`(dao.space.dht/cancel! node manifest-address)` ends a live publication now.\n   619\tIt is refused for a manifest with no live publication.\n   620\t\n   621\tEvery ledger entry is settled for **this publication** at the moment of the\n   622\tcall:\n   623\t\n   624\t| Entry at the call | Becomes | The write itself |\n   625\t|---|---|---|\n   626\t| `sent` | Stays `sent`. | Done. |\n   627\t| `failed`, nothing outstanding | `failed` with reason `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`; the earlier reason is kept under `:was`. | Its queued repair entry, if any, is removed, unless another live publication holds the address. |\n   628\t| `waiting`, queued | `failed` with reason `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`. | Its queue entry is removed, unless another live publication holds the address. |\n   629\t| `waiting` or `failed`, **request outstanding** | `failed` with reason `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`. | The request cannot be withdrawn. It runs to its fact. |\n   630\t\n   631\t- **Cancellation does not wait.** The publication is reported by the next\n   632\t  step, as `:published` if its first report had not been made and as\n   633\t  `:republished` otherwise, with `:repairing? false` and\n   634\t  `:ended :dao.space.dht/cancelled`, and it is retired. Its counts are\n   635\t  those of the table, so `:sent + (count :failed) = :blobs`.\n   636\t- **A late fact never changes a reported cancellation.** The fact of an\n   637\t  outstanding write that a cancelled publication held is applied to the\n   638\t  other live publications that hold the address, by the shared-address\n   639\t  rule, and to nothing else. The cancelled publication is retired and its\n   640\t  event is not amended.\n   641\t- **`cancelled` means not counted, not unsent.** A blob reported\n   642\t  `:dao.space.dht/cancelled` whose request was outstanding may have reached\n   643\t  peers. The result under-claims and never over-claims.\n   644\t- An address another live publication holds keeps its one queue entry and\n   645\t  its one request; that publication's accounting is untouched.\n   646\t- Nothing cancels a publication implicitly. A newer round does not\n   647\t  supersede an older one's writes.\n   648\t\n   649\t#### 5.5.6 What the result means for the index and for readers\n   650\t\n   651\t- **The announced index is unaffected.** HEAD moves when the round's blobs\n   652\t  are local and the manifest reads back, before any network result, exactly\n   653\t  as in S5. A `:partial` or `:unacknowledged` result retracts nothing.\n   654\t- **Effect on a reader.** A reader that loads an address under some\n   655\t  publication's `:failed` reaches it only if its lookup asks the publisher.\n   656\t  Otherwise its load fails `:miss` with the DHT's cause, as data. It never\n   657\t  loads wrong content and never hangs. Once repair sends the blob, a new\n   658\t  load succeeds.\n   659\t- The REPL prints the first report: the result and, when it is not\n   660\t  `:acknowledged`, the count of failed blobs, the first reason, and whether\n   661\t  the node is retrying. It prints each `:republished` the same way. Its\n   662\t  `dao.space.dht` host module exposes `retry` and `cancel` over the two\n   663\t  plain functions.\n   664\t- **A module published at the prompt** shares its round's publication. Its\n   665\t  name assertion is in the index whether or not its closure replicated; a\n   666\t  reader of a `:partial` publication may resolve the name and fail the load\n   667\t  with a `:miss`, until repair completes.\n   668\t\n  1133\t- `request-get` answering `:busy` leaves the load `:loading` and it\n  1134\t  completes on a later step. A client that cannot submit fails the load\n  1135\t  `:unaskable` with the outcome keyword, the same value on all three hosts.\n  1136\t- **Pacing.** One round of 500 puts against acknowledging peers is\n  1137\t  `:acknowledged`; the number of outstanding replicate requests never\n  1138\t  exceeds `max-pending-writes` at any step, and no `/busy` fact is\n  1139\t  produced.\n  1140\t- **Sustained rounds.** Eight consecutive rounds of 500 puts (within\n  1141\t  `:max-backlog` in total), announced faster than they drain, are each\n  1142\t  reported once, in announcement order, all `:acknowledged`; the backlog\n  1143\t  returns to empty; `busy?` then answers false. A ninth and tenth round\n  1144\t  announced before any drain exceed the bound: their excess blobs are\n  1145\t  `:dao.space.dht/backlog-full` in their first reports, which are not\n  1146\t  `:acknowledged`; repair then sends them and each round ends\n  1147\t  `:republished :acknowledged`.\n  1148\t- **The result.** A mesh that refuses the store of one chosen non-manifest\n  1149\t  blob reports `:partial` with exactly that address under `:failed`; one\n  1150\t  that refuses the manifest blob reports `:unacknowledged`. HEAD has moved\n  1151\t  in each case.\n  1152\t- **The invariant.** In every `:published` and `:republished` event of\n  1153\t  every test in this slice, `:sent` plus the count of `:failed` equals\n  1154\t  `:blobs`, and `:blobs` is the same in all events of one publication.\n  1155\t- **Repair reaches acknowledgement, from both originals.** A `:partial`\n  1156\t  original (manifest sent, one row refused): after the mesh heals and\n  1157\t  ticks pass `:repair-ticks`, exactly one `:republished` arrives with\n  1158\t  `:acknowledged`, `:sent` equal to `:blobs`, and the manifest's replicate\n  1159\t  request was issued once in total. An `:unacknowledged` original (manifest\n  1160\t  and rows refused): healing first the manifest's store, then the rest,\n  1161\t  yields `:republished :partial`, then `:republished :acknowledged`, and\n  1162\t  nothing after. A blob already `sent` is never requested again.\n  1163\t- **A peer that never accepts.** With every peer refusing or never\n  1164\t  answering stores, over sustained rounds and a long run of appended ticks:\n  1165\t  every first report is `:unacknowledged` with `:repairing? true`; **no\n  1166\t  event ever reports `:acknowledged`**; no `:republished` is produced\n  1167\t  except one `:ended :dao.space.dht/displaced` per publication beyond\n  1168\t  `:max-repairing`; the two queues together never exceed `:max-backlog`;\n  1169\t  outstanding requests never exceed `max-pending-writes`; live\n  1170\t  publications never exceed `:max-repairing`; the repair delay doubles to\n  1171\t  `:repair-max-ticks` and stays; no put ever waits; and `busy?` is false\n  1172\t  between cycles.\n  1173\t- **Fairness.** With the repair queue full of failing addresses and a new\n  1174\t  round arriving, the new round's first pass holds at least\n  1175\t  `max-pending-writes − repair-slots` outstanding and is reported while\n  1176\t  repairs continue; repair requests never exceed `:repair-slots`\n  1177\t  outstanding; with no repairs queued, a fresh round uses the whole bound.\n  1178\t- **Terminal reasons stop repair.** A publication whose only failure is\n  1179\t  `/oversize` or `/absent` is reported once with `:repairing? false` and\n  1180\t  `:ended :dao.space.dht/terminal`, and no request for it is ever issued\n  1181\t  again. One with a terminal and a retryable failure repairs the retryable\n  1182\t  one and then ends `:terminal`, `:partial`.\n  1183\t- **`retry!`** makes the next cycle run at the next step without waiting\n  1184\t  for `:repair-ticks`, produces no event of its own, and is refused for a\n  1185\t  manifest that is not repairing.\n  1186\t- **`cancel!`, in each ledger state.** A publication cancelled with some\n  1187\t  blobs sent, some queued, some failed awaiting repair, and some\n  1188\t  outstanding is reported by the next step with `:ended\n  1189\t  :dao.space.dht/cancelled`, `:repairing? false`, the sent blobs under\n  1190\t  `:sent` and every other under `:failed` as `:dao.space.dht/cancelled`,\n  1191\t  and the invariant holds. The outstanding write's `/sent` fact then\n  1192\t  arrives: no further event for the cancelled manifest is produced and its\n  1193\t  reported counts are unchanged.\n  1194\t- **Shared addresses.** Two live publications holding one address issue\n  1195\t  one request; its outcome is written to both ledgers. Cancelling the\n  1196\t  first leaves the address queued for the second, which is later reported\n  1197\t  with that address `sent`; the late fact of an address outstanding at the\n  1198\t  cancel is counted by the second and not by the first.\n  1199\t- `cancel!` before the first report produces `:published`, after it\n  1200\t  `:republished`; it is refused for a manifest with no live publication.\n  1201\t- `close!` reports nothing, discards both queues, and leaves every blob\n  1202\t  local; a node reopened on the directory issues no repair request.\n  1203\t- A solo node and a non-publishing node hold no backlog and report\n  1204\t  `/solo` and `/unpublished` in the step that reads the puts.\n# Linker over dao.jing.dht — OWNER DECISIONS (2026-10-01)\n\nInputs:\n- Lead design (fable): collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n- Second opinion (gpt-6-sol, AGREE-WITH-CHANGES): collab/1790806000000-architect-linker-over-dht-second-opinion.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n\nOwner answers (verbatim selections):\n1. Rows: \"Every round\". Materialize every evaluated program's rows into the index store each round (fable's recommendation; gpt-6-sol disagreed). This also settles the :yin/address contract: an index address fact names retrievable content.\n2. Keys: \"Loaded from a file (stable)\". A durable publisher identity, with key loss and replacement behaviour to be defined. Tests may use ephemeral keys.\n3. Dart: \"Add Ed25519 for Dart now\". Signed name resolution on JVM, Node and Dart, with cross-host signature vectors.\n4. Same name, same address from two declared principals: \"Consensus: resolve\" (the current fold behaviour). Different addresses still refuse as ambiguous.\n\nAdopted as the Architects' shared recommendation (the orchestrator stated these; the owner did not object):\n- Explicit named-export publication, derived from indexed code.\n- A configured DHT link source may start a bounded load on require; the banner and help say so; pending and failure are data.\n- Names come from explicitly loaded snapshots only.\n- Deferred: the persistent stepped ring linker, latest-root discovery, and the lease link policy.\n\n5. Repair (2026-10-01): \"Automatic retry while open\". The node re-sends failed blobs in the background, within the backlog bound, while it runs. This replaces explicit-only retry!.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The revised ledger makes cancellation and retry count against one fixed blob set, so the two earlier arithmetic conflicts are resolved. I’m checking one remaining bound claim: how many publications can remain live while their first reports are still waiting."}}
